    "body": "Kitchen Communication Role: All Kitchen Category: BOH SOP Updated: May 2026 Kitchens run on short, clear, confirmed calls. Learn the language, use it consistently, and respond to it — every time. Expediting Language These calls are used in service. Know them before your first shift on the line. Call Meaning Fire Start cooking the next course for this table now. Hold Do not fire — the table is not ready. Wait for the next call. All day Total count of a dish across all active tickets. \"Three salmon all day.\" Walking The dish is plated and being brought to the pass now. Pick up The dish is at the pass — runner is needed immediately. Window Dish is at the pass and has been there — it needs to move now. On the fly Priority re-fire — something was wrong and this replaces it urgently. Behind You are moving behind someone. Call it before you move, not after. Corner You are about to turn a corner. Always call this when carrying hot items. Hot behind You are carrying something very hot. Call it loudly and clearly. 86 Protocol When you run out Tell expo immediately — not when the next ticket arrives The moment you know a dish is 86'd — last portion used, stock not available, ingredient pulled for food safety — tell expo out loud: \"86 sea bass.\" Expo tells the manager. The manager updates FOH. FOH updates the floor. This chain must happen in under two minutes. If FOH finds out by trying to order the dish, the chain failed somewhere. Timing Between Stations Calling times Let adjacent stations know where you are If your component takes longer than others on the same ticket, tell the other stations early — not when they're already plating. Call your time clearly: \"Grill, two minutes on the rib.\" The other cook adjusts. If you don't call your time, the pass ends up with half a table and a cold plate waiting for you. Call early, call clearly, and respond when other stations call theirs. Kitchen-to-FOH Communication At the pass What to say — and what not to say — to runners When calling a runner, give the table number and any critical information only: \"Table 7, four covers.\" For allergen dishes: \"Table 5, cover 2 — no nuts, confirmed clean setup.\" Do not explain how the dish was cooked, what went wrong earlier, or give commentary. Runners relay what you say. Keep it short, correct, and repeatable. If a runner asks a question you can't answer — \"What's in this sauce?\" — the answer is to get expo or the chef, not to guess. Related BOH Home Ticket Management Pass Standards Allergen Handling Print this page"
