    "body": "Station Handover Role: All Kitchen Category: BOH SOP Updated: May 2026 A clean handover is a professional courtesy and a food safety requirement. The incoming cook should be able to take over a station without guessing at what's prepped, what's labelled, or what's running low. Step 1 Clear your current tickets before you start the handover Do not begin a station handover with open tickets on the rail. Communicate with expo: \"I have two tickets still running — 10 minutes before I can hand over.\" If the incoming cook needs to take your tickets before you're finished, walk them through each ticket verbally so there are no surprises mid-fire. Step 2 Clean and sanitize the station Wipe down all surfaces, sanitize cutting boards, and remove any food debris from the station before handover. Knives go back on the magnetic strip — not on the bench. Cloths go in the laundry, not the bin. The station the incoming cook steps into should be cleaner than the station you stepped into. Step 3 Label and date everything in mise en place Every container in your mise en place must have a full label before you leave — item name, date prepared, and use-by date. Post-it notes are not labels. Unlabelled containers must be labelled or discarded — the incoming cook is not responsible for identifying what you left. If items are at the end of their shelf life, flag them clearly or remove them from the station. Step 4 Brief the incoming cook Stand with the incoming cook and walk through the station verbally: what is prepped and where it is, what is running low and needs a prep pull before service, any recipe changes or modifications to specials that happened during your shift, any equipment issues (don't hide them), and any allergen-modified dishes that are on the current rail or expected. The verbal brief takes two minutes and prevents three hours of confusion. Step 5 Confirm handover with the kitchen manager Before you leave the section, confirm with the kitchen manager that the handover is complete. If there are open issues — low stock, equipment faults, an allergen ticket in progress — the manager needs to know before you walk out. Do not leave a station in a manager's blind spot. Related BOH Home Cold Prep Setup Date Labelling Knife Safety BOH Closing Checklist"
