    "body": "Mid-Service Reference Crisis Use this page when something is urgent. Call 911 first for immediate danger, then tell your manager. Find the closest situation below and follow the steps in order. 911 Immediate danger? Call 911 now. Do not wait for permission. Do not attempt to manage a life-safety emergency yourself. Call 911 Fire Burn Knife wound Fall Allergy Customer unwell Fridge issue Dish machine Physical Safety Fire Oil or fryer fire Turn off the heat source immediately — do not move the pan. Never use water on an oil fire. Use the fire blanket to smother it. If it does not go out within seconds, activate the fire alarm and evacuate. Call 911. Do not re-enter the building. Tell your manager once you are safely outside. Always call manager. Even if you put it out — a fire that was suppressed must be reported and the equipment inspected before it is used again. Injury Burn Cool the burn under cold running water for a minimum of 20 minutes. Start immediately. Do not apply ice, butter, oil, or any cream. Do not pop blisters or remove clothing stuck to the burn. Cover loosely with a clean non-fluffy material — not cotton wool. Tell your manager. For anything larger than a palm, call 911. Always call manager. All burns are recorded in the accident log before end of shift. Injury Laceration or knife wound Apply firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth. Hold it — do not keep checking. Elevate the hand or arm above heart level if possible. If bleeding is severe, does not slow within 5 minutes, or the wound is deep — call 911. Tell your manager immediately. Do not return to food handling until the wound is fully covered with a blue detectable plaster. Always call manager. Blue detectable plasters are in the first aid kit. If the plaster comes off during service, stop and replace it immediately. Injury Slip, trip, or fall Do not move the person if they hit their head or complain of neck or back pain. Call 911. If they are conscious and there is no head or spinal concern, help them to a safe position. Tell your manager immediately. Identify and address the hazard — wet floor, spilled oil — before anyone else walks through it. Always call manager. All falls are recorded in the accident log. The hazard that caused it is your manager's responsibility to address — flag it clearly. Food Safety Allergen Customer flags an allergy after ordering Stop the ticket immediately. Do not let the dish reach the pass. Tell your manager or head chef now — before anything else. Do not guess whether the dish is safe. The answer comes from someone who knows the recipes and prep. If the dish has already been served, tell your manager before the customer eats further. Do not attempt to remove an allergen from a dish that already contains it. Discard and remake. Always call manager. An incorrect \"it should be fine\" is the most dangerous thing you can say in this situation. Your uncertainty is not the problem — acting on it without checking is. Food Safety Customer reports feeli"
