    "body": "Line Cook Your First Week Role: Line Cook New Hire Path Updated: May 2026 This is your path through the first week. Not a general orientation — a day-by-day reading list specific to this role that tells you exactly what to read, in what order, before you touch anything. Follow it in sequence. Each SOP takes about three minutes to read and will make your shift less confusing than if you hadn't read it. At the end of the week, you sign off to confirm you've read and understood everything. That sign-off goes to your manager. It is not a test. It is a record that you were given the information. Before Your First Shift Three reads. Ten minutes. Do this before you arrive. These three SOPs are non-negotiable starting points. They cover hygiene, allergens, and knife handling — the three areas where a mistake on day one causes a serious problem. Read them before you walk in. You will be expected to know them. Read in order 01 Handwashing Procedure ~2 min 02 Knife Safety & Storage ~3 min 03 Allergen Handling ~4 min By the time you arrive for your first shift, you should be able to name the Big 9 allergens from memory, explain why cross-contact is different from cross-contamination, and know where knives are stored and how they are passed safely. Day One Observe the shift. Ask questions. Do not freelance. Your first shift is about orientation, not output. You will shadow your trainer through service. Watch how the station is set up, how handoff happens between positions, and how communication runs on the pass. You are not expected to work independently. You are expected to be present, ask questions when you do not understand something, and not improvise. You will be shown the physical layout of everything. Confirm each of these with your trainer before service starts: Where knives are stored and how they go back Where the probe thermometer is kept and how to calibrate it Where allergen information is posted and how to flag a query How food is labelled in the walk-in — what goes on a label and what format Where the handwashing station is and what is always kept stocked there How a ticket moves from the printer to the pass At the end of day one, you should know where everything is. You do not need to be fast. You need to know where things live. Day Two Learn your station before service starts. Before your second shift, read these three SOPs. They cover your station setup, the fryer, and grill cleaning. On day two you will begin to assist with setup under supervision. You will not be running a station independently — but you will be expected to understand what correct looks like so you can do it right when your trainer steps back. Read before this shift 04 Cold Prep Setup ~4 min 05 Fryer Startup & Shutdown ~3 min 06 Grill Cleaning Protocol ~3 min By the end of day two, you should be able to set up the cold prep station without being told what goes where, and explain the correct sequence for fryer startup and shutdown. Your trainer will check both before they s"
