    "body": "Opener Your First Week Role: Opener New Hire Path Updated: May 2026 The opener arrives before anyone else and leaves the kitchen ready for service. When that work is done correctly, nobody notices. When it is not — a missed temperature log, a fryer not at temp, stock that hasn't been rotated — everyone downstream pays for it during service. This path gives you the reads and the orientation you need to open independently by the end of your first week. Follow it in order. Sign off at the end. That form confirms to your manager that you have the information. Before Your First Shift Three reads before you arrive. The opener is the first person responsible for food safety compliance each day. Temperature logging, handwashing, and storage rotation happen before anyone else gets in. Read these three SOPs before your first shift so you understand what you are accountable for when you walk through the door. Read in order 01 Handwashing Procedure ~2 min 02 Temperature Logging ~3 min 03 Dry Storage ~3 min Before you arrive, you should understand what a temperature log records, how often it is completed, and what the legal minimum requirements are. You should also know what FIFO means and why it is the opener's responsibility to check rotation before service starts. Day One Shadow the full opening sequence in order — do not skip steps. Your first shift is observation. You follow the current opener through every task, in the exact sequence they do it, and you take note of the order. The opening sequence has a logic to it — temperatures are checked before equipment is started, equipment is started before food is pulled, food is pulled before the station is set. Understand why the order matters, not just what the order is. Confirm all of the following with your trainer before you leave after day one: Where the temperature log lives and how to fill it in correctly Which fridges and freezers are logged, and in what order What to do if a fridge reading is outside the safe range when you arrive Where the probe thermometer is stored, how it is calibrated, and who to tell if it is missing How dry storage is checked — what correct rotation looks like and what to flag Where the opening checklist form is submitted and who receives it By the end of day one, you should be able to describe the full opening sequence from memory and know where every piece of equipment you will operate is located. Day Two Equipment startups — fryer and station setup. The fryer and cold prep station are the two systems you start and confirm are ready before service. Read both SOPs before your second shift. On day two, you will perform these startups with your trainer present. You will not do anything unsupervised yet — but you will be expected to know the correct sequence before you touch the equipment. Read before this shift 04 Fryer Startup & Shutdown ~3 min 05 Cold Prep Setup ~4 min By the end of day two, your trainer should be confident that you can light the fryer correctly, confirm it re"
