    "body": "Burrata & Tomato Category: Starter Station: Cold Updated: May 2026 Yield 8 portions Portion 1 burrata Pickup 3 min Station Cold Contains Milk Gluten Sulphites Ingredients Per 8 portions 8 Burrata, drained but kept moist 1.2 kg Mixed tomatoes, ripe, cut unevenly for texture 120 ml Basil oil 80 ml Sherry vinaigrette 8 slices Sourdough, sliced for toast to finish Maldon salt, basil leaves, cracked black pepper Step 1 Cut and season tomatoes close to service Cut tomatoes no more than 2 hours before service. Use a mix of wedges, halves, and thick slices so the plate has height and texture. Season with salt and sherry vinaigrette, then stand for 15 minutes. Drain excess liquid before plating. Step 2 Temper burrata and set cold station Remove burrata from the fridge 15 minutes before service and keep covered. Set tomatoes, basil oil, vinaigrette, basil leaves, Maldon, black pepper, and sourdough on the cold station. Keep spare burrata in the fridge until needed. Burrata should be cool, not fridge-hard. Do not leave dairy at room temperature longer than needed for service. Step 3 Toast sourdough to order Brush sourdough lightly with oil and toast until crisp at the edges but still easy to bite. Season with a little salt. Do not plate soggy or cold toast; it should be the final component before the dish leaves. Plating Tomatoes first, burrata off-centre, oil around Place drained tomatoes in a low pile. Set burrata slightly off-centre and tear the top gently so the centre is visible. Spoon basil oil around the plate, finish with basil leaves, Maldon, and black pepper. Serve toast on the side. Storage & Shelf Life Burrata Keep in original liquid below 41 F. Use within 24 hours of opening. Tomatoes Cut tomatoes are same-day only. Do not hold dressed tomatoes overnight. Basil oil Keep chilled and labelled. Use within 3 days. Related Recipe Library Cold Prep Setup Allergen Handling"
