    "body": "Receiving & Inspection Role: Opener / Manager Updated: May 2026 Category: Food Safety Never sign a delivery note before inspecting the goods. Once you sign, you accept responsibility for the condition of everything in that delivery. Inspect first, then sign — or reject what fails. Step 1 Check the delivery vehicle and driver Before unloading, check that the delivery vehicle is clean, refrigerated units are running, and there is no evidence of pests or contamination inside the vehicle. The driver should be able to confirm the temperature readings for chilled and frozen goods during transit. If the vehicle is clearly unclean or temperature-compromised, reject the delivery and contact your manager immediately. Step 2 Inspect packaging for damage, labelling, and use-by dates Before moving product into storage, inspect every case or box. Look for: torn or wet packaging, evidence of freezer burn or thawing, damaged seals on vacuum-packed items, missing or illegible use-by dates, and any signs of pest activity. Reject items with missing date labels — you have no way to determine safe use-by. Note — Check allergen labelling on any new or substitute product from the supplier. If the product has changed and allergen information differs from what you expected, notify the head chef before storing or using it. Step 3 Probe-check temperatures of chilled and frozen goods Take a probe temperature of at least one item from each chilled category received. Insert the probe between sealed packages — do not pierce packaging to probe. Chilled goods must arrive at or below 46 F (ideally 41 F or below). Frozen goods must arrive solidly frozen — any partial thaw or significant ice crystals may indicate temperature abuse in transit. Note — Record the probe temperature for each category on the Receiving Log . If a chilled item arrives above 46 F, reject it and log the rejection. Step 4 Reject non-compliant goods immediately Any item that fails on temperature, packaging, labelling, date, or quality must be set aside and clearly marked \"REJECTED.\" Do not mix rejected items with accepted stock. Contact the supplier to arrange collection or credit. Document all rejections on the Receiving Log with the reason for rejection and the action taken. Reject criteria summary: Chilled goods above 46 F on arrival Frozen goods showing signs of thaw Missing or expired use-by date Damaged, torn, or compromised packaging Evidence of pest activity (droppings, damage, odour) Unusual color, texture, or odour in fresh produce or protein Step 5 Store immediately and rotate FIFO Accepted items must be moved into correct storage immediately — do not leave chilled goods sitting in the receiving area. Rotate stock using FIFO: new stock goes behind or below existing stock so older product is used first. Apply date labels to any unlabelled prep items before storing. See the Dry Storage SOP and Date Labelling SOP for storage order and labelling rules. Note — Raw proteins must always be stored below rea"
